zing public safety and technological integrity would champion such a framework, empowering federal regulators with clear audit authority before deployment.\n\nAbsent these independently verified safety mechanisms, granting limited liability would dangerously incentivize unchecked deployment, eroding the foundational trust and accountability that underpin stable societies. History is replete with examples where de-risking developers without robust, independent oversight led to systemic harm, from the early pharmaceutical industry's tragic failures to financial deregulation that triggered global economic crises. This 'move fast and break things' mentality, when applied to foundational technologies, does not foster sustainable innovation; it invites synthetic chaos into our most critical systems, making the public bear the cost of developer negligence. This approach risks creating a moral hazard, where developers benefit from reduced liability while the public pays with direct harm and the erosion of institutional stability. True innovation thrives within clear boundaries and accountability, not in an unregulated frontier. The political will exists to demand accountability for technologies that impact daily life, just as it does for food, drugs, and financial markets.",
